WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.713
This is an assembly line for water pumps: a row of stations,

2
00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:08.103
workplaces where one fixed part of the work is done.

3
00:00:09.097 --> 00:00:14.008
A conveyor, a moving belt or chain, carries the product from station to station.

4
00:00:15.026 --> 00:00:17.650
Each station adds something: the pump housing,

5
00:00:17.894 --> 00:00:20.228
the impeller, the cover with six screws,

6
00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:23.049
and finally the test: then the pump is done.

7
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:26.667
Follow one product and count the stations.

8
00:00:27.389 --> 00:00:31.742
Ask at each one: what is added here, and does a person or a machine do it?

9
00:00:33.111 --> 00:00:36.176
A manual station is a person with hand tools,

10
00:00:36.327 --> 00:00:38.370
like here at the housing and the cover.

11
00:00:39.374 --> 00:00:44.111
Semi-automatic: the person loads and starts, and a machine does the work.

12
00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:47.373
This press pushes the impeller into place.

13
00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:51.844
Fully automatic, the machine does it all, like this test,

14
00:00:52.041 --> 00:00:56.708
behind guarding: a light curtain stops the machine when someone reaches through.

15
00:00:57.721 --> 00:01:02.040
And by the stack light, the lamp column on the machine: usually green running,

16
00:01:02.226 --> 00:01:03.979
yellow waiting, red a fault.

17
00:01:04.756 --> 00:01:06.230
That can differ per plant.

18
00:01:07.588 --> 00:01:11.210
The cycle time is how long a station takes for one product.

19
00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:16.811
With a stopwatch: 50, 55, 65 and 45 seconds.

20
00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:22.099
The slowest station is the bottleneck: the whole line cannot go faster.

21
00:01:22.844 --> 00:01:24.806
Products pile up in front of it.

22
00:01:25.844 --> 00:01:28.491
Takt is the time per product to meet demand.

23
00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:32.816
Seven hours is twenty-five thousand two hundred seconds,

24
00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:36.765
divided by 420 pumps: 60 seconds.

25
00:01:37.771 --> 00:01:45.667
The cover station, at 65 seconds, is above takt: 387 pumps per shift, 33 short.

26
00:01:46.456 --> 00:01:47.989
That is where you look first.

27
00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:52.393
In a paced line the belt moves everything on together,

28
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.701
on the takt: every station must finish in time.

29
00:01:56.716 --> 00:02:00.396
In an unpaced line each station passes on when it is done,

30
00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:05.562
usually on pallets over a belt that keeps running, with small queues in between.

31
00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:09.887
A cell is a compact group of machines, often in a U,

32
00:02:10.026 --> 00:02:12.534
with a few operators who walk with the work.

33
00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:17.404
Cells suit many variants, paced lines high, stable volumes.

34
00:02:18.754 --> 00:02:23.572
Four stations of about 40 seconds, with room for 1 product between them.

35
00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:28.596
Station 3, a press-fit unit, sometimes jams for 4 minutes.

36
00:02:29.596 --> 00:02:31.697
Without buffers the line is hard-linked:

37
00:02:31.871 --> 00:02:34.762
stations 1 and 2 cannot pass their product on,

38
00:02:34.925 --> 00:02:36.597
station 4 gets nothing.

39
00:02:37.322 --> 00:02:40.143
So faster stations 1 and 2 do not help.

40
00:02:41.252 --> 00:02:43.237
Then the availabilities multiply:

41
00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:46.673
eight stations that can each run 95 percent

42
00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:49.680
of the time give about 66 percent together.

43
00:02:50.691 --> 00:02:53.686
A buffer is a small queue between two stations.

44
00:02:54.387 --> 00:02:59.751
Size it on the stop: 240 seconds divided by 40 is 6 places.

45
00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:03.379
Buffers absorb stops, they do not remove them.

46
00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:06.200
Now stations A to D.

47
00:03:06.894 --> 00:03:12.072
Press B takes 30 seconds, leak tester C 36: C is the bottleneck,

48
00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.685
with a buffer of 5 places in front.

49
00:03:15.457 --> 00:03:18.116
B stops, and C works on from the buffer.

50
00:03:19.178 --> 00:03:23.845
Station A has finished a product, but the place after it is full: A is blocked.

51
00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.752
If the stop lasts longer than 3 minutes, the buffer is empty.

52
00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.507
C could work, but nothing arrives: C is starved.

53
00:03:33.842 --> 00:03:36.605
A buffer only works if it can fill up again.

54
00:03:37.302 --> 00:03:42.747
B makes 120 an hour, C 100: that difference is the spare capacity.

55
00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:48.303
The buffer refills at 20 an hour: 5 places take 15 minutes.

56
00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:54.802
With a stop every 20 minutes, B runs about 17 minutes in between, just enough.

57
00:03:55.479 --> 00:03:57.000
Stops every ten minutes?

58
00:03:57.775 --> 00:03:59.006
Then attack the stops.

59
00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:05.739
All products in the stations and buffers together are the WIP, the work in progress.

60
00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:10.065
So protect the bottleneck first: the buffer before it full,

61
00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:14.489
against starved, the buffer after it nearly empty, against blocked.

62
00:04:15.866 --> 00:04:20.672
A discrete-event simulation plays every product, stop and repair in time,

63
00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:21.927
with the real spread.

64
00:04:22.943 --> 00:04:26.670
MTBF is the average running time between two stops.

65
00:04:27.691 --> 00:04:30.454
MTTR is the average length of a stop.

66
00:04:31.162 --> 00:04:35.550
Does that stop data come from machine data, or is it a guess by the supplier?

67
00:04:36.598 --> 00:04:42.461
An integrator promises 112 an hour against a target of 105.

68
00:04:43.141 --> 00:04:48.319
But in his model every station stops for exactly 3 minutes every 2 hours.

69
00:04:49.485 --> 00:04:54.895
Real stops last 30 seconds to 20 minutes, and fixed stops flatter the buffers.

70
00:04:55.722 --> 00:05:00.053
Ask for real stop data, and validate the model on an existing line.

71
00:05:01.356 --> 00:05:06.116
The quick check is Little’s law: WIP equals throughput times lead time.

72
00:05:07.097 --> 00:05:13.262
The model holds about 40 products, at 112 an hour: 0.36 hour,

73
00:05:13.425 --> 00:05:15.608
about 21 minutes in the line.

74
00:05:16.292 --> 00:05:17.952
Close to the planned 20.

75
00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.691
After the first few places,

76
00:05:20.854 --> 00:05:24.709
bigger buffers often give a little more output but much more lead time.

77
00:05:25.345 --> 00:05:27.655
So ask: where does the output level off?
